Staged, NOT applied, because qc7 is running and these files are what its row subprocesses execute.
Apply only when no run is in flight:

    cp host/staged/{coolgate.sh,tpu-run.sh,local-run.sh,quality-compare.sh,google-lane-run.sh} host/ \
      && rm host/staged/APPLY-PENDING

WHY THIS ROUND EXISTS: I edited the live host/coolgate.sh while qc7 was mid-run. Both arm runners
SOURCE it at startup, so rows after the edit executed a file the run's recorded runner digest did not
describe. The live file was restored, the window and the affected rows are recorded in that run's own
directory, and the general fix is below.

  coolgate.sh         calls through an _cg_adb adapter instead of $ADB directly. The caller may define
                      it; if not, the string form is assumed, which is what the in-VM runners want.
                      google-lane-run.sh holds adb as an argv ARRAY, and the old bridge --
                      ADB_SAVE="${ADB[*]}" then ADB=("${ADB_SAVE}") -- collapsed `adb -s SERIAL` into a
                      single executable name containing a space, which failed at the NEXT command.
  google-lane-run.sh  die() is defined FIRST. It was called from `cool_gate || die` before it existed,
                      so on a hot phone bash printed "die: command not found" and carried on -- there
                      is no errexit -- and the whole batch ran and recorded nocool=0. The gate also
                      moved INTO the row loop: one gate before 24 rows says nothing about rows 2..24.
                      gate=per-row is in the settings string and therefore in every key, so batch-gated
                      results cannot be served as per-row-gated ones.
  quality-compare.sh  FREEZES the harness, CONTENT-ADDRESSED. Two defects in the first attempt, both
                      reproduced independently: only the CPU arm ran the frozen copy (the TPU arm still
                      invoked ./tpu-run.sh live, so row 01 tagged SOURCE=ORIGINAL and row 02
                      SOURCE=EDITED-LIVE under one identity), and `rm -rf $OUT/harness` destroyed the
                      earlier run's frozen copy on reuse and could delete one another invocation was
                      executing from. Now: staged into a private directory, digested BY BASENAME, and
                      published at $OUT/harness/<RUNNERS_ID>. Same harness -> same directory, verified
                      and reused; different harness -> its own directory, the older one untouched.
                      Nothing is deleted. BOTH arms execute the frozen copies. harness=frozen is in the
                      key. A .producer.lock is taken BEFORE BUILD or MANIFEST is truncated, so two runs
                      cannot share an OUT and erase each other; a lock from a dead pid is reclaimed.

Enforced by tpu/test/{coolgate,key-binding,runners-matched,google-gate}-test.sh, which check this
directory while this marker exists and host/ once it is gone.
